WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.170
Film Courage: What makes a story boring?

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.080
David Willis, writer/producer: What makes
a story boring?

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.500
Uggh….anything with superheroes.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.500
Sorry!

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.670
I don’t like superhero movies.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.059
Film Courage: Why?

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:15.290
David Willis: I sat down and watched like
a dozen of them once.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:16.290
I might as well look at these.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.680
I’m not giving these a fair shot.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.440
A guy running around in tights…if you’re
running around in tights you should either

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:31.460
be sword fighting in the 16th century or in
a ballet (ballet is great).

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.550
But I just never buy into it.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.160
They’re fun, I get why people like them.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.590
I figured out why they are popular even though
I’m not the audience because it’s replacing

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.060
belief.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.890
The single largest believing demographic in
the United States under the age of 30 are

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:59.190
the non-believers and so faith is having less
importance in life in America today and I

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.420
think it’s being replaced with the mythology
of the superheroes because you get to see

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.191
the ancient Roman Gods, the ancient Greek
Gods, they had a whole pantheon…and now

00:01:08.191 --> 00:01:13.600
this has taken its place…and you can dress
up like them and you can go to Comic Con and

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.960
you can engage in your “faith.”

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.970
I don’t know…that’s my theory.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.830
That’s my crazy writer theory.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.430
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.430
So then a non-superhero movie.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.300
What makes a non-superhero movie boring?

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.850
Take that out of the equation?

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.890
David Willis: What makes it boring?

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.130
Film Courage: Or a story in general?

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:35.200
I know you talked earlier about it before
before, not enough conflict or passive characters?

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:42.250
David Willis: I think a story is boring if
it doesn’t matter.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.070
If there are no stakes.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:53.060
If the people in the movie, if they don’t
care a lot about what is happening (what they

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.900
are doing), then the audience won’t care.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.640
That’s to me what makes a story boring.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:08.970
If you are watching a character and they need
$10,000.00, why do they need $10,000.00?

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:14.330
Are they paying for their child’s operation
to save their life, that’s important.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.590
I’d be like “Oh, that matters.”

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.770
Are they just trying to buy a car so they
can go to work?

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:30.190
Okay, that’s a real thing I guess but so
what, you know?

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:38.090
James Bond, does he have to harm this guy
because the guy took his parking space or

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.490
is he doing it to save the world.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:49.530
The French can make movies about being boring
and make those work.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:55.930
There are certain indie films where you can
actually delve into that in a deeper way.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:03.350
But for me that’s it, if the stakes aren’t
there, if the story…if the hero succeeds

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.270
or doesn’t succeeds or whatever, why does
it matter.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.720
If it doesn’t matter, I don’t care.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.630
Film Courage: So for you when you go to see
a film and I know you said lately you’ve

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.489
been so busy with all this [his success crowdsourcing
campaign] you don’t even have time to watch

00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:21.450
television but before all of that could you
tell in the first few minutes if something

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:22.800
is going to be boring?

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.980
David Willis: My girlfriend (bless her heart)
has to listen to me after the first few minutes

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.110
say “This is what this movie is about”
because I just can’t help it.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.970
But like we recently saw THEIR FINEST.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:44.340
THERE FINEST is a British film about a woman
who is hired to make propaganda films in WWII

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:52.599
in England and in the first few minutes of
that movie they bring you to London (WWII)

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:53.599
under the blitz.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:59.770
There are things coming down and there is
this young woman who needs a job because her

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:04.819
husband is having a hard time getting hired.

00:04:04.819 --> 00:04:11.800
And so she needs a job and she goes into the
office (the propaganda film office of the

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:18.440
British government) they call her in and the
stakes are there for two reasons: if she doesn’t

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:26.139
get a job she and her husband are in trouble
and if she doesn’t make good propaganda

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:36.460
films, the public that is being bombed may
feel more bleak.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.229
Whereas if she does a good job with these
films, they will feel better.

00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:46.650
Now we know how WWII ends so…who wins here?

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:52.810
But we don’t know how individual people
felt in London at the time being bombed (so

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.900
that’s a mystery, we’re going to find
that out).

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.869
So immediately the first 5-6 minutes of the
film you care if she gets this job and if

00:05:00.869 --> 00:05:02.620
she’s good at it.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.490
Film Courage: What was it about those stakes?

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:11.669
Because I mean the storyline sounds great
but if someone else did it and the stakes

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:16.909
weren’t there…there must have been little
nuance about how they drew you in that made

00:05:16.909 --> 00:05:20.319
you sit up on the edge of your seat a little
bit?

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.169
David Willis: Well, they had several kinds
of stakes.

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:29.930
First of all: The War, the big stakes, the
broad stakes, the moral of London.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.270
The morale of England, those were the big
stakes.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:38.680
Then they had the personal stakes: she was
a woman who brought her in…this film office

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.669
was run by all men, okay.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:43.590
And they were very dismissive of her.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:48.419
They were told by a higher up that the propaganda
films that they were making that the female

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:54.699
dialogue was terrible and so they said “Well,
let’s bring in this girl and she’ll write

00:05:54.699 --> 00:05:55.699
the female bits.”

00:05:55.699 --> 00:05:59.330
And they bring her in and they’re very dismissive
and “We’ll do all the rest of the work

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.330
my dear.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.629
You do all of the female bits.”

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:03.029
And they were very dismissive of her.

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:13.590
So right away there is this chauvinism appropriate
for the time and you’re like “Hey, that’s

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:14.629
not right…2017.”

00:06:14.629 --> 00:06:17.479
So there are these immediate personal stakes,
as well.

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:23.169
So they added a layer to it that was threaded
throughout the movie.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:26.370
But it wasn’t just all about girl power.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.960
They moved beyond that later in the movie
and made it even deeper.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:30.960
Film Courage: Sure.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.531
But those different layers (it sounds like)
drew you in.

00:06:33.531 --> 00:06:40.949
There were the higher stakes, then her stakes
at home and then you add this combative environment

00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:42.759
that she has to go into.

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:44.410
So there were many things?

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:45.759
David Willis: Right.

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:50.419
Film Courage: When you see a movie that within
the first 5 minutes it falls flat, are we

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:53.539
only seeing one layer?

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:54.539
Is that sometimes why?

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:55.539
David Willis: [Shakes his head].

00:06:55.539 --> 00:06:57.439
Film Courage: It sounds like we really can’t
break it down to a science?

00:06:57.439 --> 00:07:02.479
David Willis: Yeah, it if was a science you’d
have computers do it.

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:04.689
Nobody knows what’s going to be a hit and
what isn’t.

00:07:04.689 --> 00:07:09.639
And there are so many factors in making a
movie.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:17.849
So many factors from the script to what the
directors doing with the, and the cast.

00:07:17.849 --> 00:07:20.889
One actor may make a character sympathetic.

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:23.909
Another different actor may make them annoying.

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:27.679
There are just so many factors.

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:33.589
By the way, I like the name of your website
(Film Courage).

00:07:33.589 --> 00:07:40.069
I’m curious how you came up with that because
it takes courage to make a movie.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:45.069
Even if you make a bad movie…even if you
make a bad movie…it still takes courage.

00:07:45.069 --> 00:07:49.039
I’m not talking about the fake kind of courage
that Apple has when they take away your headphone

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:55.939
jack on the iPhone but it does take courage
and to make a good movie takes even more courage.

00:07:55.939 --> 00:07:58.490
You have to take a chance, you have to go
out on a limb.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.599
You have to do something that hasn’t been
tried before because if we’ve seen it all

00:08:02.599 --> 00:08:06.429
before, why do we want to see it again?

00:08:06.429 --> 00:08:08.139
Film Courage: Why does it take courage?

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:14.209
Because it seems like if it’s in you and
it [The Story] just pours out, wouldn’t

00:08:14.209 --> 00:08:15.619
nothing stop you?

00:08:15.619 --> 00:08:19.889
Or is it putting it out in the public space
and holding it up to the light for scrutiny,

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:21.460
that’s where the courage comes into play?

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.619
David Willis: Well, that definitely but to
do it in the first place…but to go into

00:08:24.619 --> 00:08:29.569
show business in the first place, on the face
of it is a stupid idea.

00:08:29.569 --> 00:08:37.780
300 people move to Los Angeles every day to
become actors, okay.

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:46.150
That is a million people over 10 years who’ve
come here just to be actors.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:52.490
Now the odds…if you just look at the numbers…the
odds of you becoming successful as an actor

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:53.780
are terrible.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:57.090
As a writer, they are terrible.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.220
So if you look at it logically you say “Well,
I’m not going to do that.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:00.600
I’m going to become a doctor.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:01.600
I’m going to do something else.”

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.550
Because there is a very clear path.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:08.060
I get my degree, I go to medical school, I
become a doctor.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:11.030
It’s clear, it’s logical, it’s reasonable.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:18.890
So to even go into show business take courage
because the odds are you’re going to fail

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:24.160
so the people who make it are the ones who
can’t do anything else.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:31.130
And by that I mean, they won’t get out of
bed for anything else, they just can’t.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.430
They have to do it.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.420
They are driven to do it.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:38.160
Those are the ones who don’t give up and
keep going because they can’t do anything

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.160
else.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:39.380
They just keep doing it.

